{"text": "Memorandum\non the\nelimination of fiscal barriers\n\nas envisaged\nin the Commission's White Paper on completing the Internal Market\n\n\u2018The Commission's White Paper suggests to abolish custans controls\nat intra-community borders and\n- to replace it by a \"Clearing House\u201d system as regards VAT,\nmaking controls at borders superfluous;\n\n= to provide \"sufficient\" approximation of the excise duty rates\nin order to make frauds pointless.\n\n\u2018The European Council has invited the Ministers of Finance to study\nthe whole chapter of the White Paper relating to the removal of fiscal\nbarriers and to study,\n\n- whether the various measures proposed are necessary and\n\nappropriate, \u00b0\n- if so, the appropriate timing.\n\n\u2018The European Council has therefore not approved the Commission's approach\nbut did not disapprove of it either. Nevertheless, certain governments\nand in particular the UK goverment has voiced strong objections.\n\nI hear fron the Council's secretariat that the study of this chapter\nwill deal! in the first place with the assumptions and\u2019 principles on\nhich the Commission's approach is based. There are apparently doubts\nin the minds of certain experts that the envisaged scheme e.g. for\nvaT can really work in relation between all Menber States. It works\nsatisfactorily between the Benelux countries which are neighbouring\ncountries, but it might not be working in relationsbetween Dennark\nand Greece at least not in the foreseeable future given the consider-\nable differences in structure and quality of the internal adninistrat-\njon and general attitudes of both countries.\n\n2.\n\nSeevezgtzoz",
